{% comment %}
  Is this order cancelled? One place answers it, for every pass that counts.

  Input:  cancelled_order  the order object being counted.
  Output: customer_order_limit_cancelled  true when the order must not count.

  EasyStore does not expose cancellation under one name. This theme's own pages
  prove it: the order list and the account page read `order.is_cancelled` as an
  integer (`== 1`), while the order detail page reads `order.cancelled`, both as
  a boolean and against 0. The order objects a product or cart page receives are
  not the ones the account templates render, so a pass that reads only
  `is_cancelled` sees nil on the shape that carries `cancelled`, treats the order
  as live, and counts a cancelled order against the customer's allowance. That is
  the bug this snippet exists to close, so every known spelling is read here and
  nowhere else.

  Only a positive signal cancels an order. A missing, blank, 0 or false field
  says nothing, so an order whose shape carries none of these fields still counts
  and the limit stays enforced: an unreadable order is never a free allowance.

  Values are normalized to lowercase strings before comparison because the flag
  arrives as an integer, a boolean or a label depending on the field, and Liquid
  treats 0 as truthy — an `unless` on a raw value skipped every order, which is
  how this pass once counted nothing at all.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
  The order is read through one resolved reference. `include` shares the caller's
  scope on EasyStore, so the loop variable `order` is already visible here; the
  passed input is preferred and the ambient loop variable is the fallback, which
  keeps this snippet correct whether or not the platform binds an object passed as
  an include parameter. Every caller loops with `order`, and the input is cleared
  at the end so a stale one cannot be read for the wrong order.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled_order = cancelled_order | default: order %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled = false %}

{% comment %} The integer flag, as the order list and account page read it. {% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag = customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.is_cancelled | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase %}
{% if customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == '1' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'true' %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled = true %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %} The boolean, as the order detail page reads it. {% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag = customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.cancelled | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase %}
{% if customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == '1' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'true' %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled = true %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  A cancellation timestamp. Any readable value is a cancellation date; the
  falsy spellings are listed because an engine may render an absent field as one
  of them rather than as an empty string.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag = customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.cancelled_at | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase %}
{% unless customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == '' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == '0' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'false' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'nil' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'null' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'none' %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled = true %}
{% endunless %}

{% comment %}
  The status the order is filed under. `order.status` is the account order list's
  own tab filter — the order detail page links back to `?filter={{ order.status }}`
  — so a cancelled order carries the cancelled tab's status. The American
  spelling is read alongside it because the status strings are not this theme's.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag = customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.status | default: customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.order_status | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase %}
{% if customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'cancelled' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'canceled' %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled = true %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  The payment and fulfillment labels, which the order pages render as "Cancelled".
  A refund is deliberately not read here: a refunded order was still placed, and
  whether it frees an allowance is a merchant decision, not a field name.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag = customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.financial_status_label | default: customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.financial_status | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase %}
{% if customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'cancelled' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'canceled' %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled = true %}
{% endif %}

{% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag = customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.fulfillment_status_label | default: customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.fulfillment_status | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase %}
{% if customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'cancelled' or customer_order_limit_cancelled_flag == 'canceled' %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_cancelled = true %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  A compact record of what this order actually exposed, for the diagnostics pass
  to sample. Verifying the field names on a live store is otherwise a guess.
{% endcomment %}
{% capture customer_order_limit_cancelled_signals %}is_cancelled={{ customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.is_cancelled | default: '' }} cancelled={{ customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.cancelled | default: '' }} cancelled_at={{ customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.cancelled_at | default: '' }} status={{ customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.status | default: '' }} financial={{ customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.financial_status_label | default: customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.financial_status | default: '' }} fulfillment={{ customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.fulfillment_status_label | default: customer_order_limit_cancelled_order.fulfillment_status | default: '' }}{% endcapture %}

{% comment %}
  The input is cleared so a caller that forgets to pass an order cannot inherit
  the previous one. `include` shares the caller's scope on EasyStore, and reading
  a stale order here would answer for the wrong order.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign cancelled_order = '' %}
